CORRECTION (supersedes this patch's WHY below): the mechanism described
here -- a lost gfx7/8 EOP completion interrupt -- is not what causes the
hang this patch was written for. Both call sites wait with
HSA_WAIT_STATE_ACTIVE, and BusyWaitSignal::WaitRelaxed and
InterruptSignal::WaitRelaxed alike treat ACTIVE as a pure spin on
signal_.value; hsaKmtWaitOnEvent_Ext is only reached on the passive path.
No interrupt participates in the wait, so no interrupt can be lost from
it. The real cause is the missing GFXIP 7/8 double-mapped ring -- see
patches/rocm-systems/aql-ring-queue-full-workaround.patch.

That makes this mitigation not merely useless but actively harmful: it
gives up on a packet that is still live in a ring the CP has not drained,
and lets the process free the kernarg and signal the CP may still write
into. That is the "device lost from bus" cascade and both full-box hard
lockups recorded in RESOLVED_VRAM_MARGINALITY_INVESTIGATION.md. It stays in the tree,
still off by default, only because it was validated against a real vLLM
workload before any of this was understood. Do not enable
ROCR_GFX8_EOP_MITIGATION or ROCR_GFX8_EOP_MITIGATION_HIP_TIMEOUT_US.

SECOND CORRECTION (2026-08-29): the ring double-map fix pointed to above
also does not fix the hang -- see RESOLVED_VRAM_MARGINALITY_INVESTIGATION.md's top-of-file
summary. The hang is VRAM-clock marginality (mining-tuned VBIOS driving
Hynix VRAM above its rated speed), confirmed on hardware by both dropping
MCLK to 1750MHz on the mining VBIOS and by flashing a correct-vendor
Hynix-strapped VBIOS at its stock 1750MHz ceiling -- 64/64 and 75/75 clean
runs respectively, versus repeated same-boot hangs before. Not a ring,
EOP, or software bug at all.

Original header follows.

